  "content": {
    "title": "UALR announces Ford’s hire as men’s basketball coach",
    "text": "The University of Arkansas at Little Rock named Travis Ford its next men’s basketball coach in a news release Thursday morning. UALR hired Ford after ESPN reported March 19 that the deal was being finalized.UALR will hold an introductory news conference for Ford at 11 a.m. next Tuesday at the Jack Stephens Center on campus in Little Rock. Ford will become the 24th head coach in program history.“I want to sincerely thank Chancellor Christina Drale and (Athletic Director) Frank Cuervo for their trust and belief in me to lead this program,” Ford said through the Thursday release. “I’m grateful for the opportunity to be a part of this university and to represent this community.”Ford has 24 years of coaching experience at the Division I level, with his most recent stop being at Saint Louis from 2016-24. Prior to leading the Billikens, Ford coached Oklahoma State from 2008-16. He also coached at UMass from 2005-08 and Eastern Kentucky from 2000-05.Ford guided Saint Louis to a 146-109 record during his eight-year tenure. He led the Billikens to four 20-win seasons and an appearance in the 2019 NCAA Tournament after winning the Atlantic 10 Conference Tournament that year.Ford compiled a 155-111 record at Oklahoma State in the eight seasons prior. The Cowboys reached the NCAA Tournament five times under Ford but went just 1-5, the lone win coming in his first season.Ford coached UMass to a 62-35 record in three seasons and led Eastern Kentucky to a 61-80 mark in five years. He has a career record of 491-355 over 27 seasons total, which includes his first coaching stop at Campbellsville University in Kentucky, an NAIA school, from 1997-2000.“Travis Ford brings the veteran leadership and proven track record of success that the Trojans need to become consistent winners,” Drale said. “We are confident he will represent the university with excellence while leading our men’s basketball program to sustained success.”As a player, Ford began his college career at Missouri in the 1989-90 season then transferred to Kentucky. He played for the Wildcats from 1991-94 under Rick Pitino and reached the Final Four in 1993.Ford earned a first-team All-SEC selection as a junior and a second-team pick as a senior. He was named the Southeast Region’s Most Outstanding Player in the 1993 NCAA Tournament, as well as the Most Valuable Player of the SEC Tournament in both 1993 and 1994.Ford has not coached the past two seasons after being fired by Saint Louis in 2024. He was hired to replace Darrell Walker, who was fired by Cuervo following an eight-year run. UALR has not made an NCAA Tournament since 2015-16, Chris Beard’s only season as coach before leaving for Texas Tech.“I’m excited to connect with our fans, alumni and the entire Central Arkansas community,” Ford said. “We want them to feel a part of what we’re building and to make the Jack Stephens Center a place that’s electric on game nights. We’re going to build something special here.”Walker compiled a 113-133 record in his eight seasons at UALR, including a 12-20 mark in 2025-26. Despite winning two regular-season conference championships in 2020 (Sun Belt) and 2024 (Ohio Valley), the Trojans never reached the NCAA Tournament under Walker.UALR earned a bye into the semifinal round of the conference tournament as the No. 1 seed in 2020, but it was canceled after the quarterfinals because of covid-19. It again earned the No. 1 seed and a bye into the semifinals in 2024, but lost to third-seeded Morehead State 69-55 in the OVC title game with a spot in the NCAA Tournament on the line.In a news conference March 6, the day Walker was fired, Cuervo said the program needed “a breath of fresh air” moving forward.He said the expectation is for UALR to be a consistent contender as it transitions into the United Athletic Conference next season.“We want to relentlessly pursue on-court competitive excellence,” Cuervo said. “For us, that means competing annually for conference championships in the new United Athletic Conference, which we’ll join on July 1, and positioning ourselves to be able to compete in the NCAA Tournament.”",
